8.4 CRITICAL FIX — GEOGRAPHICALLY ACCURATE GOVERNORATE LABEL POSITIONING

The current map has a serious label-positioning problem: several governorate names and their markers appear outside their actual geographical boundaries or are incorrectly positioned.

Fix the underlying positioning logic, not just the visual appearance.

Requirements:

1. Use the actual geographic polygon geometry of each governorate to determine its correct label position.
2. Associate every governorate label and marker with its correct governorate data. Never assign approximate or arbitrary coordinates.
3. Place each governorate name inside its corresponding geographical boundary whenever reasonably possible.
4. Calculate suitable label positions using reliable geographical methods, such as polygon interior points or appropriate label-point algorithms.
5. Do not simply use polygon bounding-box centers when they may fall outside irregular governorate shapes.
6. For small or closely spaced governorates, use intelligent collision avoidance and carefully designed label placement without confusing their geographical associations.
7. Prevent labels from appearing outside Iraq's national boundaries.
8. Prevent labels and markers from overlapping neighboring governorate names, map boundaries, or important visual elements.
9. Keep every label centered horizontally and vertically within its associated label background.
10. Ensure label positions remain correct when the map is resized, zoomed, or displayed on different mobile and desktop screen sizes.
11. Preserve original geographical boundaries and proportions. Never distort or move governorate polygons to accommodate labels.
12. If a label cannot reasonably fit inside a small governorate, use a short, subtle leader line connecting the label to its correct geographical location. Keep the label close to its governorate and make the association unmistakable.
13. Verify all 19 governorate labels individually against the actual geographical data.
14. Ensure markers, labels, and governorate polygons use the same geographical coordinate reference system and correct map transformations.
15. Do not manually shift labels to visually convenient but geographically incorrect positions.

Final acceptance requirement

Every governorate name must clearly identify the correct governorate, remain visually connected to its actual geographical location, and never appear randomly outside the map.

Test all 19 labels individually on mobile screens before declaring the issue fixed.

